error[E0425]: cannot find function `bv8` in module `crate::m`
  --> $DIR/clean_origin_bitvec_widths.rs:82:23
   |
LL |     let _ = crate::m::bv8(1u8);
   |                       ^^^ not found in `crate::m`
   |
note: `bv8` is declared in this `clean { … }` island, but it did not become callable from Rust
  --> $DIR/clean_origin_bitvec_widths.rs:73:9
   |
LL |         def bv8  (x : BitVec 8)  : BitVec 8  := x
   |         ^^^
   = note: `bv8` is kernel-checked and argument 0 carries TOTALLY and exactly (`BitVec 8` -> `u8`), but this compiler cannot make `bv8` CALLABLE from Rust, because it has no way to re-earn the emitted body's agreement with the declaration at `BitVec 8`.
           Why, precisely: the faithfulness gate reflects the EMITTED MIR back into Clean using only the emitted body's own local types — deliberately, so that an emitter which typed a local wrongly is caught — and the Clean domain it reads off `u8` is `UInt8`, never `BitVec 8`. `UInt8` and `BitVec 8` are DIFFERENT Clean types (`UInt8` is a structure whose one field is a `BitVec 8`), so the reflected term is not definitionally equal to the declaration and the gate refuses. Refusing HERE turns that into a diagnostic at the call site with options, instead of a fail-closed correctness refusal at code generation.
           This is a measured boundary of the compiler, not a rule about what you may write (ruling v7/D2), and it is NOT a soundness problem: nothing wrong is emitted, and `BitVec 8` still carries everywhere a carrier is only READ — contract clauses, citations, and the declared-carrier lane.
           options:
           - restate `bv8` at `UInt8`/`UInt16`/`UInt32`/`UInt64`, which has the SAME Rust carrier at the same width and is emittable today, and keep the `BitVec 8` version alongside for proof position if the bit-level spelling is what the proof wants;
           - cite `bv8` from `requires`/`ensures` instead of calling it — proof position needs no emitted body and no reflection;
           - write the computation as a Rust `fn` and prove it agrees with `bv8` in an `ensures` clause, which is the same guarantee reached from the other side;
           - or extend the compiler: side B needs a reflection domain whose Clean type is `BitVec 8` at the Rust carrier `u8`, AND a way to SELECT it that does not read the declaration. Unsigned `BitVec`-typed domains already exist in `trust_spec_elab` (`Domain::U128` at `u128`, `Domain::USize` at `usize`), so the missing half is not signedness: it is that `ty_to_domain` maps the emitted local's Rust type ALONE — deliberately, and pinned by `h_i_the_reflector_never_sees_the_plan` — so nothing may tell it that this `u8` stands for `BitVec 8` rather than `UInt8`. That is an owner decision with four costed options, written up in `docs/design/2026-09-14-bitvec-return-direction.md`; it is not a widening someone should just write.

